{
  "image_name": "2b4330a99d6362134b2f63e06a8a7573.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes parent-of-origin gene regulation at the 11p15 imprinted region, showing how maternal and paternal chromosomes differ across two linked domains. In the telomeric domain, the maternal allele expresses **H19**, while the paternal allele expresses **IGF2**. In the centromeric domain, the maternal allele expresses genes such as **KCNQ1** and **CDKN1C**, whereas the paternal allele is associated with expression of the long noncoding transcript **KCNQ1OT1**. These opposite expression patterns are tied to differentially methylated regions (DMRs), indicated by filled versus open circles.\n\nThe lower schematic zooms in on the H19/IGF2 control region and highlights the mechanism more explicitly. On the maternal chromosome, unmethylated binding sites allow **CTCF** to bind near the H19 region. This appears to insulate **IGF2** from shared enhancers, so enhancer activity supports maternal **H19** expression instead. On the paternal chromosome, methylation prevents CTCF binding at the same sites, removing the insulator effect. As a result, enhancers can activate **IGF2**, leading to paternal **IGF2** expression while **H19** is silenced. Overall, the figure illustrates classic genomic imprinting controlled by allele-specific methylation and chromatin insulation.",
